==English==
{{wp}}
{{wikidata lexeme|L324428}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|noobie}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:der<unc>|newie|:blend<unc>|newborn|baby|:der<unc>|new|boy|tree=+}}
{{unc|en}} — perhaps an alteration of {{m|en|newie}} with [[intrusive]] {{m|en||b}} (compare {{m|en|freebie}}); possibly a {{blend|en|newborn|baby|nocap=1}}; or perhaps a shortening of {{m|en|[[new]] [[boy]]}} or {{m|en|[[new]] [[beginner]]}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈnjuːbi/|/ˈnuːbi/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-newbie.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːbi|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[newcomer]] or [[neophyte]], someone [[new]] to something. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en| He was a '''newbie''' to chemistry.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Scottie Andrew, Allen Kim and Tiffany Baker|title=How to talk about the Grammys best new artist nominees like a pro|url=https://www.cnn.com/interactive/2020/01/entertainment/62nd-grammy-awards-best-new-artist-trnd/|date=2020-01-23|passage=We’ve included some other acts you already know so you’ll get a feel for what the '''newbies''' sound like.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250312100458/https://www.cnn.com/interactive/2020/01/entertainment/62nd-grammy-awards-best-new-artist-trnd/|archivedate=12 March 2025}}
# {{lb|en|Internet}} A [[new]] [[user]] or [[participant]]; someone who is extremely new and [[inexperienced]] (to a game or activity). A beginner.
# {{lb|en|slang}} Anything recently introduced into a [[setting]], especially something that replaces an older [[version]].

====Usage notes====
* The term "newbie" was greatly popularized with the advent of the Internet, but was in use before then.

